---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

// Prompts kept as consts so literal braces / angle brackets render verbatim
// (.astro treats { } as expressions — a const string sidesteps all escaping).
const buildPrompt = `Build me a scheduled QBR Prep agent. Do all of this end to end:

1. Confirm the WYRE MCP Gateway works and the systems are reachable: it must
   expose the autotask_*, datto_*, cipp_*, liongard_* and IT Glue tools.
2. Pick ONE designated client to prepare the QBR brief for, and discover its
   five IDs - these get baked into the routine prompt so the routine never runs
   discovery itself:
   - Autotask companyID via autotask_search_companies.
   - Datto RMM site UID (the site the client's devices live in).
   - CIPP tenant ID via cipp_list_tenants, matched by company name/domain.
   - Liongard environment ID via liongard_environments_list, matched by name.
   - IT Glue organization ID via search_organizations.
   If the client cannot be matched in one system, record that - the routine
   degrades that section gracefully.
3. Confirm a Slack connector is connected. Note the destination channel name
   and ID (e.g. #sw-dev). If Slack does not show in the /schedule connector
   list, read its connector_uuid and url from an existing routine that already
   uses Slack (RemoteTrigger list -> get -> mcp_connections).
4. Create a Claude-managed scheduled routine named "QBR Prep":
   - Schedule: weekly, cron "0 14 * * 1" (Monday 10:00 America/New_York =
     14:00 UTC). Faster-than-hourly cadences are rejected.
   - Attach TWO connectors, each with permitted_tools populated:
       * WYRE MCP Gateway: autotask_search_tickets, datto_list_devices,
         cipp_list_users, cipp_list_mfa_users, liongard_detections_list,
         search_documents, create_document, update_document_section
       * Slack: slack_send_message
     An empty permitted_tools list = the routine runs with no tools.
   - Routine prompt: every run, draft a QBR conversation brief for the
     designated client at levels 3–5 of the MSP Value Pyramid (Ease of Doing
     Business, Emotional Value, Transformational Value). Do not produce a
     ticket review. Operational reads are optional proof for those levels, and
     raw figures go in an appendix labeled "do not present as the agenda."
     Write the brief to an IT Glue document, and post a one-line summary to
     Slack. Use the exact routine prompt below, with your client's five IDs
     baked in.
5. Confirm the create response echoes both connectors with the correct
   permitted_tools and the routine prompt verbatim. Do not trigger a run -
   routine runs need a one-time approval handled separately.`;

const routinePrompt = `You are the QBR Prep routine for WYRE. Once a week you draft a Quarterly Business Review conversation brief for one designated client. The brief is for an account manager talking to an SMB owner. Coach levels 3–5 of the MSP Value Pyramid (Pisces Consulting, adapted from Bain & Company's B2B Elements of Value):

- Level 3, Ease of Doing Business: working with the MSP cost the owner less time, effort, and surprise.
- Level 4, Emotional Value: the owner worries less, looks competent, and trusts that someone competent already has this.
- Level 5, Transformational Value: technology lets the business do something it could not do before.

You do not produce a ticket review. Most MSPs communicate only level 1 (Table Stakes) and level 2 (Functional Value): ticket counts, SLA percentages, patch percentages, threats blocked. Those numbers are not the agenda. Pull operational data only as optional proof for a higher-level point. File raw figures in an appendix so they are not presented.

All IDs are baked in below - do NOT call any list_*/search_* discovery tool to find IDs (the only allowed search is search_documents to find-or-create the IT Glue doc).

Designated client: <client name>
- Autotask companyID: <autotask-company-id>
- Datto RMM site UID: <datto-site-uid>
- CIPP tenant: <cipp-tenant-id>
- Liongard environment ID: <liongard-environment-id>
- IT Glue organization ID: <itglue-org-id>

Read the four signals below. If a call fails, put "data not available" in the appendix only - never omit the appendix line silently, and never promote a failure into a talking point you invented.

1. Tickets. Call autotask_search_tickets with companyID <autotask-company-id> and createdAfter set to 90 days ago. Do not report volume, priority mix, or open count as a finding. Keep at most one pattern that shows the owner had to chase, ask twice, or manage a change themselves (level 3). If you cannot name what the owner had to do, write "no level 3 proof in tickets."

2. Devices. Call datto_list_devices with siteUid <datto-site-uid>. Ignore the large udf block on each device. Keep a device fact only when it shows something that never became the owner's problem (level 4) or a constraint blocking a business move you can name (level 5). Otherwise it stays in the appendix.

3. Identity. Call cipp_list_users and cipp_list_mfa_users with tenantFilter <cipp-tenant-id>. Keep an identity fact only as level 4 (an exposure that did not land on the owner) or level 5 (a control standing between them and a customer, a hire, or a site). A coverage percentage is not a talking point.

4. Configuration changes. Call liongard_detections_list with a startDate 90 days ago, pageSize 5, and a filter for EnvironmentID <liongard-environment-id>. Read only the most recent ~15 detections and only their small metadata fields (ID, SystemName, Name, Date, SystemType). Never read the large ChangeDetection field. Keep a change only when it is a surprise the owner would have felt (level 3) or drift that blocks a named business move (level 5).

5. Assemble the conversation brief, in this order. This is the document. The appendix is last and is not the QBR.
   - Floor check (levels 1–2): one sentence. "The floor held. Do not walk tickets, SLA, patch, or threat counts." Or name the single thing the owner would have felt. No tables.
   - Level 3 — Ease of Doing Business: at most four sentences in owner language, at most one proof point, and two questions. One question is always "Where did you still have to chase us?"
   - Level 4 — Emotional Value: one moment that did not become the owner's problem, and two questions. One question is always "What about IT still wakes you up?"
   - Level 5 — Transformational Value: questions that ask what the business is trying to make true this year, plus at most one roadmap item in this shape — the business move, what becomes true for the owner, what we would change in one paragraph (not a SKU list), what we need from them, the horizon, and what we will not do. If these reads do not reveal a business goal, say the goal is unknown and do not invent a transformation, an AI roadmap, or a hardware refresh.
   - Do not say: the metrics and openers that would collapse the meeting back into a ticket review.
   - Appendix — Supporting evidence, do not present as the agenda: ticket volume and how many are still open, device count and health concerns (antivirus not RunningAndUpToDate, offline/stale check-in, reboot pending), user count and MFA coverage, and a short list of recent configuration changes.

6. Deliver. Call search_documents with organization_id <itglue-org-id> and name 'QBR Pack - <client name>'. If a matching document is found, use update_document_section to write the new brief into it. If not found, use create_document with organization_id <itglue-org-id>, name 'QBR Pack - <client name>', and the brief as HTML content. If the IT Glue org or document step fails, fall back to including the full brief inline in the Slack message. Then call slack_send_message to channel <channel-id> (#sw-dev) with: 'QBR conversation brief ready for <client name> — levels 3–5 drafted; floor: <one line>'. Do not put ticket, device, or MFA counts in the Slack line.

Do not invent data or a business goal. If a tool returns nothing or errors, say so in the appendix.`;
---
<DocsLayout title="QBR Prep" description="Build a weekly QBR conversation brief for one client — levels 3–5 of the MSP Value Pyramid, not a ticket review. Operational reads from Autotask, Datto RMM, CIPP, and Liongard are an evidence appendix. The brief is written to IT Glue and a one-line summary goes to Slack.">
  <h1>QBR Prep</h1>

  <p class="lead text-lg text-[var(--muted)] mb-8">
    A worked example of an <a href={`${baseUrl}advanced-workflows/`}>advanced workflow</a>:
    a Claude-managed scheduled agent that, once a week, drafts a Quarterly Business
    Review <em>conversation</em> for one designated client. The meeting spine is levels
    3–5 of the MSP Value Pyramid — Ease of Doing Business, Emotional Value, and
    Transformational Value — not a ticket review. Reads from Autotask, Datto RMM, CIPP,
    and Liongard are optional proof for those levels. Raw counts sit in an appendix
    labeled so nobody presents them. The brief is written to an IT Glue document, and a
    one-line summary goes to Slack. There are no servers and no code to deploy: the
    agent is a saved prompt plus a schedule plus two MCP connectors, running in Claude's
    cloud.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-builds">What it builds</h2>
  <p>
    The finished workflow runs unattended on a weekly cron. Each run it drafts the
    brief an account manager can take to an SMB owner: a one-sentence floor check,
    a level 3 story and questions, a level 4 story and questions, and a level 5
    question set with at most one business-move roadmap item. Ticket volume, device
    health, MFA coverage, and configuration changes are gathered only to support or
    reject those points, then filed in an appendix. The same four sections always
    appear in that appendix, so a reader can tell "nothing to cite" from "we could
    not check."
  </p>
  <p>
    The in-room coaching for this brief — including when to refuse a metric, and when
    to stop and ask a human — lives in the gateway agent
    <code>qbr-prep-agent</code>. This page is the scheduled collector that must follow
    the same rule: do not run a ticket review.
  </p>

  <hr class="my-8" />

  <h2 id="pyramid">The meeting this brief is for</h2>
  <p>
    The MSP Value Pyramid (Pisces Consulting, adapted from Bain &amp; Company's B2B
    Elements of Value) scores how an MSP talks about value. Most quarterly reviews
    never leave the first two levels.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Level</th><th>Name</th><th>What the owner hears</th><th>In this brief</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>1</td>
        <td>Table Stakes</td>
        <td>You showed up. Tickets were answered, systems were watched.</td>
        <td>One sentence, or silence. Not a section.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>2</td>
        <td>Functional Value</td>
        <td>The service did its job. Uptime held, threats were blocked.</td>
        <td>One sentence, or silence. Not a section.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>3</td>
        <td>Ease of Doing Business</td>
        <td>Working with you cost me less time, effort, and surprise.</td>
        <td>Story, one proof point, questions.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>4</td>
        <td>Emotional Value</td>
        <td>I worry less. Someone competent already has this.</td>
        <td>One moment that did not become theirs, questions.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>5</td>
        <td>Transformational Value</td>
        <td>The business can do something it could not do before.</td>
        <td>Questions, and a roadmap item only if a business move is real.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <hr class="my-8" />

  <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>
  <p>Before building, your Claude account needs all of the following:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>WYRE MCP Gateway connector</strong></td>
        <td>Connected in claude.ai (<code>https://conduit.wyre.ai/v1/mcp</code>). Provides the <code>autotask_*</code>, <code>datto_*</code>, <code>cipp_*</code>, <code>liongard_*</code> and IT Glue tools. See the <a href={`${baseUrl}getting-started/gateway/`}>Gateway overview</a>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Autotask, Datto RMM, CIPP, Liongard &amp; IT Glue enabled in the gateway</strong></td>
        <td>The gateway's API users must be able to <strong>read</strong> tickets, devices, users/MFA, detections and organizations — and to <strong>create</strong> and <strong>update</strong> IT Glue documents. These reads feed the appendix and the occasional proof point. They are not the agenda.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Slack connector</strong></td>
        <td>The first-party Slack connector connected in claude.ai (<code>https://mcp.slack.com/mcp</code>). Provides <code>slack_send_message</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Scheduled routines access</strong></td>
        <td>Created via the <code>/schedule</code> capability; managed at <a href="https://claude.ai/code/routines" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">claude.ai/code/routines</a>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>A designated client present across those systems</strong></td>
        <td>One client that can be matched in Autotask, Datto RMM, CIPP, Liongard and IT Glue, so all five IDs can be baked into the routine prompt.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>A destination Slack channel</strong></td>
        <td>e.g. <code>#sw-dev</code>, plus its channel ID. The Slack connector must have access to it.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <hr class="my-8" />

  <h2 id="gotchas">Known gotchas</h2>
  <p>
    These are the things that cost real time the first time through. Account for them
    up front and the build genuinely takes minutes.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The cadence floor is hourly.</strong> Scheduled routines reject
      faster-than-hourly cron expressions. A QBR brief is a weekly artifact anyway —
      <code>0 14 * * 1</code> runs it every Monday morning.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>permitted_tools</code> must be populated per connector.</strong> A
      routine with a connector attached but an empty <code>permitted_tools</code> list
      runs with <em>no tools</em> and silently does nothing — no error, no output. List
      the exact tool names the routine needs, and only those.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>QBRs are quarterly and staggered per client — a routine is a cron.</strong>
      Real QBRs land on a per-client cadence, not all at once. This POC sidesteps that by
      preparing a brief for <em>one designated client</em> every week. The production
      pattern is a "QBR radar" routine that scans the PSA for clients with a QBR due in
      roughly two weeks and drafts a brief for each — but that needs a source of QBR dates
      to scan, which is out of scope here.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A scheduled run cannot know the owner's business goal.</strong> Level 5
      without that goal is fiction. The routine must say the goal is unknown and stop
      at the questions, rather than invent a transformation from ticket or device data.
      The account manager adds the goal before the meeting. That coaching is
      <code>qbr-prep-agent</code>, not this cron.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Liongard's metric-evaluation endpoint is unavailable.</strong> The
      configuration read uses <code>liongard_detections_list</code> — recent change
      detections — instead. Window it to the last ~90 days with a small page size, read
      only the most recent ~15 detections' metadata, and never read the large
      <code>ChangeDetection</code> field.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ignore the Datto <code>udf</code> block.</strong> Each
      <code>datto_list_devices</code> record carries a 300-field <code>udf</code> block
      that is almost entirely empty. For one client's site that is a single call and
      manageable — just tell the routine to ignore the <code>udf</code> block.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A routine reaches only its attached connectors.</strong> The routine
      sandbox blocks arbitrary network egress, so notifications must go through the
      Slack connector's tools. Attach every connector the workflow touches.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <hr class="my-8" />

  <h2 id="build-prompt">The one-shot build prompt</h2>
  <p>
    With the connectors above in place, paste this to Claude. It confirms the gateway,
    discovers the designated client's five IDs, and creates the routine.
  </p>
  <pre class="not-prose"><code>{buildPrompt}</code></pre>

  <hr class="my-8" />

  <h2 id="routine-prompt">The resulting routine prompt</h2>
  <p>
    This is the lean prompt the build process installs into the scheduled routine itself.
    Substitute your designated client's five IDs and destination channel. The prompt
    tells the routine to draft levels 3–5 and to keep operational counts in the appendix.
  </p>
  <pre class="not-prose"><code>{routinePrompt}</code></pre>

  <hr class="my-8" />

  <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
  <h3>The brief is the pyramid, not the four systems</h3>
  <p>
    A QBR that leads with ticket volume, device health, MFA coverage, and configuration
    changes is a level 1–2 status report. The routine still calls those systems —
    Autotask, Datto RMM, CIPP, and Liongard — because a higher-level point sometimes
    needs one concrete proof. Each read is kept in the brief only when it answers
    "what did the owner have to do?" (level 3), "what never became their problem?"
    (level 4), or "what business move does this block?" (level 5). Everything else
    is appendix. All five client IDs are baked into the routine prompt, so the routine
    never spends a run on discovery.
  </p>
  <h3>The IT Glue document is updated in place</h3>
  <p>
    Each run the routine searches the client's IT Glue organization for a document
    named "QBR Pack - &lt;client name&gt;". If it exists, the routine updates it; if not,
    it creates it. Reruns therefore converge on a single living document rather than
    piling up new ones — the latest brief is always at the same place, and the Slack
    message is just a pointer to it. The Slack line names the floor check, not a
    ticket or MFA count.
  </p>
  <h3>Graceful per-section degradation</h3>
  <p>
    If any one system can't be read — a client unmatched in CIPP, a Liongard timeout —
    that line of the appendix carries a "data not available" note instead of vanishing.
    The appendix always has the same four lines, so a reader can tell the difference
    between "nothing to cite" and "we couldn't check." A missing read is not filled
    with a different metric, and it is not promoted into a level 3–5 story. If the
    IT Glue step itself fails, the routine falls back to posting the full brief inline
    in Slack.
    See <a href={`${baseUrl}advanced-workflows/delivery-adapters/`}>delivery adapters</a>
    for other ways to surface a routine's output.
  </p>

  <hr class="my-8" />

  <h2 id="extending">Extending it</h2>
  <p>
    The natural next step is the <strong>QBR radar</strong>: instead of one designated
    client on a fixed weekly cron, a routine that scans the PSA for clients with a QBR
    due in roughly two weeks and drafts a level 3–5 brief for each one. That needs a
    source of QBR dates to scan against — a custom field, a calendar, a tracking sheet —
    which is why it is out of scope for this POC. Widen the appendix only when the new
    read can prove a higher level. A financials section, via the <code>qbo</code>
    connector, belongs here only as evidence for a level 5 move the owner already
    named (a second site, a hire, a customer that requires a control) — not as another
    dashboard in the meeting.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm text-[var(--muted)]">
    Questions or a workflow you'd like documented?
    <a href="https://github.com/wyre-technology/msp-claude-plugins/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open an issue</a>
    in the <code>msp-claude-plugins</code> repository.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
